:"woe!","H7667":"breaking","H2470":"weak","H4347":"wound","H589":"I","H389":"surely","H2088":"this","H2483":"sickness","H168":"tent","H7703":"to ruin","H4340":"cord","H5423":"to tear","H1121":"child","H5750":"still","H6965":"(Leb)-kamai","H3407":"curtain","H7462":"House of Shepherds","H1875":"to seek","H3651":"right","H7919":"be prudent","H4830":"pasturing","H6327":"to scatter","H8052":"tidings","H2009":"behold","H7494":"quaking","H6828":"north","H7760":"make","H5892":"excitement","H3063":"Judah","H8077":"devastation","H4583":"habitation","H8577":"jackal","H3045":"to know","H376":"man","H1980":"went","H6806":"step","H3256":"to discipline","H4941":"judgement","H639":"anger","H6435":"lest","H4591":"to diminish","H8210":"pour","H2534":"rage","H4940":"family","H7121":"call to","H398":"to eat","H3615":"finish","H5116":"pasture","H8074":"destroyed"},"ccc":{"1":[2112],"2":[2112],"3":[2112],"4":[2112],"5":[2112],"6":[2112],"7":[2112],"8":[2112],"9":[2112],"10":[2112],"11":[2112],"12":[2112],"13":[2112],"14":[2112],"15":[2112],"16":[2112]},"cccp":{"2112":"__P7E#"},"com":[["challoner",23,null,"challoner","The way of a man is not his.... The meaning is, that notwithstanding man’s free will, yet he can do no good without God’s help, nor evil without his permission. So that, in the present case, all the evils which Nabuchodonosor was about to bring upon Jerusalem, could not have come but by the will of God."],["haydock",2,null,"haydock","Ways. Religious rites. v. 3. — Fear. Worship. Lev. xix. 14. — Signs. They were designed to point out the seasons. Gen. i. 14. (Calmet) — Heathens thought that they had a divine power. (Worthington)"],["haydock",4,null,"haydock","Asunder. Is. xliv. 8. Bar. vi. 3. Wisd. xiii. 11. The pagans themselves laughed at such folly. (Calmet) — If such things were gods, the makers and tools deserved more adoration: Fabri deorum vel parentes numinum, says Prudentius. The vanity of such idols was more easily discerned than that of the stars. (Worthington)"],["haydock",5,null,"haydock","Tree. The ancient statues were ill-formed, (Diodor. 4.) before Dedalus brought the art to greater perfection, and gave them a living attitude."],["haydock",6,null,"haydock","There. Septuagint omit the three following verses; and from v. 9. all these, &c. to the end of v. 10. which Grabe inserts in a different character. (Haydock)"],["haydock",7,null,"haydock","Wise. MS. 2. has in the margin \"kings,\" perhaps more correctly. (Kennicott)"],["haydock",8,null,"haydock","Vanity, and shews them clearly to be foolish (Calmet) and wicked. (Haydock)"],["haydock",9,null,"haydock","Ophaz, or Phison, (Gen. ii. 11.; Calmet) the coast of Pegu, Faprobana, &c. (Menochius)"],["haydock",10,null,"haydock","Wrath. God is the true cause of all that terrifies mankind."],["haydock",11,null,"haydock","Heaven. This verse is in Chaldee, for the captives to use at Babylon, to defend themselves. It should be in a parenthesis, as it interrupts the discourse. Perhaps it was added during the captivity."],["haydock",13,null,"haydock","Voice. Thunder, (Ps. xxviii. 3.) which is usually the forerunner of rain. Lightning is occasioned by the inflamed exhalations of conflicting clouds."],["haydock",14,null,"haydock","Knowledge. If it were real, he would see the vanity of idols. v. 8. (Calmet)"],["haydock",16,null,"haydock","Portion. The Lord. H. Ps. lxxii. 6. — Rod, to measure, (Ps. lxxiii. 2.) or the sceptre and ruler. (Menochius)"],["haydock",17,null,"haydock","Shame. Idols. See if they will keep thee. Hebrew \"thy merchandise,\" or most precious effects."],["haydock",18,null,"haydock","Found by the enemy. Hebrew also, \"find\" me, being taught by affliction. C. xxix. 13. (Calmet) — Entering into sentiments of penance, they shall say, Woe, &c. (Haydock)"],["haydock",22,null,"haydock","North. He has announced this conqueror eight times. — Dragons. Tannim means \"huge reptiles,\" whether of sea or land. (Calmet)"],["haydock",23,null,"haydock","The way, &c. Notwithstanding man's free-will, yet he can do no good without God's help, nor evil without his permission. So that, in the present case, all the evils which Nabuchodonosor was about to bring upon Jerusalem, could not have come but by the will of God. (Challoner; Worthington) — This prince succeeds by thy order. (Theodoret) — Yet chastise us as a father, and have us not to his fury. v. 24. (Calmet) — \"Let new preachers blush, who say that each one is governed by his own will,\" (S. Jer. C. ix. 23.) and able to do good without God's grace. (Haydock)"],["haydock",24,null,"haydock","Judgment. C. xxx. 11. Ps. vi. 1. Justice is not contrary to mercy. (Calmet)"],["haydock",25,null,"haydock","Glory. This is repeated from Ps. lxxviii. 6. (Menochius) — The prophet supposes that these nations will not repent, and that their sins are more enormous. To see God's people treated with greater severity, might scandalize the weak, while infidels would take occasion to abuse their own impunity. (Calmet)"]],"au":{"challoner":["Richard Challoner",0,0,1781,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/03566a.htm"],"haydock":["George Leo Haydock",0,0,1849,"https://www.newadvent.org/cathen/07160a.htm"]},"fa":[[6,null,0,"Exposition of the Christian Faith","Book 1",0,397,"Therefore we ought to believe that God is good, eternal, perfect, almighty and true, such as we find him in the Law and the Prophets and the rest of the Holy Scriptures, for otherwise there is no God. For he who is God must be good, seeing that fullness of goodness is of the nature of God. God, who made time, cannot be in time. Again, God cannot be imperfect, for a lesser being is plainly imperfect, seeing that it lacks somewhat whereby it could be made equal to a greater. …"],[6,null,0,"Exposition of the Christian Faith","Book 2",0,397,"These tokens so declare the nature of the Son, that by them you may know both that the Father is eternal, and that the Son is not diverse from Him; for the source of generation is He Who is, and as begotten of the Eternal, He is God; coming forth from the Father, He is the Son; from God, He is the Word; He is the radiance of the Father's glory, the expression of His substance, the counterpart of God, the image of His majesty; the Bounty of Him Who is bountiful, the Wisdom of Him Who is wise, the Power of the Mighty One, the Truth of Him Who is true, the Life of the Living One."],[11,null,1,"Homilies on 1 Corinthians","Homily 20",1,407,"Next, he adduces what indeed is the greatest token of divinity; \"of Whom are all things.\" For this implies also that those others are not gods. For it is said, \"Let the gods who made not the heaven and the earth perish.\" Then he subjoins what is not less than this, \"and we unto Him.\" For when he saith, \"of Whom are all things,\" he means the creation and the bringing of things out of nothing into existence. But when he saith, \"and we unto Him,\" he speaks of the word of faith and mutual appropriation, as also he said before, \"but of Him are ye also in Christ Jesus.\""],[11,null,2,"Reply to Faustus the Manichaean","Book 13",2,430,"… He would see the idols of the nations perishing from off the earth, and he would find that this is predicted by the prophets, as in Jeremiah, \"Then shall ye say unto them, The gods that have not made the heavens and the earth shall perish from the earth, and from under heaven;\" and again, \"O Lord, my strength, and my fortress, and my refuge in the day of affliction, the Gentiles shall come unto Thee from the ends of the earth, and shall say, Surely our fathers have inherited lies, vanity, and things wherein there is no profit. Shall a man make gods unto himself, and they are no gods? …"],[14,null,2,"Expositions on the Psalms","Psalm 48",3,430,"But haply for a time as the false gods. For because they can be called gods, but cannot be so, for a time they are even called so. For what saith the Prophet, or what warneth He to be said to them? This shall ye say to them, \"The gods that have not made the heavens and the earth, even they shall perish from the earth, and from those that are under the heavens.\" He is not such a god: for our God is above all gods. Above all what gods? \"For all the gods of the nations are idols, but the Lord made the heavens.\" The same then is our God. \"This is God, even our God.\""],[14,null,2,"Expositions on the Psalms","Psalm 99",3,430,"He said, \"The gods that have not made the heavens and the earth, let them perish from the earth, and from under the heaven. He said not, The gods that have not made the heavens and earth, let them perish from the heaven and from the earth; because they never were in heaven: but what did he say? \"Let them perish from the earth, and from under the heaven. As if, while the word earth was repeated, the repetition of the word heaven were wanting (because they never were in heaven): he repeateth the earth twice, since it is under heaven. \"Let them perish from the earth, and from under the heaven,\" from their temples. …"]],"fau":[["ambrose","Ambrose of Milan",1,"father",null,1,397],["chrysostom","John Chrysostom",1,"father",null,1,407],["augustine","Augustine of Hippo",1,"father",null,1,430]],"ftr":[["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 2, vol. 10 (1896)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf210"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 1, vol. 12 (1889)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf112"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 1, vol. 4 (1887)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf104"],["Nicene and Post-Nicene Fathers, series 1, vol. 8 (1888)","https://ccel.org/ccel/schaff/npnf108"]]}